        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We started from the treadmills on this site whose figures we have already cross-checked against
            the maker and a second source, and kept only those with a built-in touchscreen — not a
            tablet holder, and not an LCD readout. We then wanted spread on the three questions buyers
            actually ask: how big a screen, which platform, and how much of it works without paying. That
            gave us two iFIT machines, one each from Bowflex, Echelon and Peloton, and Sole's
            no-membership F80.
          </p>
          <p>
            We judged the treadmill first and the screen second. A large screen on a weak treadmill is a poor
            buy, so every pick here has at least a 20 by 60-inch running surface and incline, and we note
            where a machine's rating, motor or warranty is the limit. We left out machines whose current
            figures we could not confirm, and models that were not on sale in the US on the day we checked.
            None of this involved testing: these are editorial judgements from the makers' figures,
            retailer listings and independent coverage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recalls were part of the check. The Consumer Product Safety Commission's database lists two 2021
            Peloton recalls of the original Tread and Tread+, which preceded the current models; iFIT recalls
            of NordicTrack <a href="/best-rowing-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>rowing machines</a> (2025) and NordicTrack and ProForm adjustable dumbbells; and
            Bowflex dumbbell and home-gym recalls. None covers a treadmill on this page. We found no recalls
            for Echelon or for Sole treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Screen Size: What 10, 16 and 24 Inches Get You</h2>
<p>
            Touchscreens on current home treadmills come in roughly three sizes, and the size you need depends
            on what you will look at.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>10 inches</strong> (<a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>NordicTrack T Series</a> 10, Sole F80 at 10.1) is
            about the size of a standard tablet. It is enough for a class video, a route map and your metrics
            at arm's length, and it keeps the price down. It is small for watching a film from the back of the
            belt.</li>
            <li><strong>16 inches</strong> (<a href="/bowflex-t16-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Bowflex T16</a>, NordicTrack Commercial 1750) is the sweet spot for
            most buyers who mean to use the screen every session. Video is comfortable at running distance,
            and on-screen controls are big enough to hit while moving.</li>
            <li><strong>22 to 24 inches</strong> (Echelon Stride-8s at 22, Peloton Tread Vision at 23.8, and NordicTrack's <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial
            2450</a> and X24 at 24) is a small television. It matters most if you stream films or follow
            off-treadmill strength classes, which is why the larger screens tend to swivel or pivot.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Movement matters as much as size. A tilting screen, as on the T Series 10, angles up or down for
            your height. A pivoting or swivelling screen, as on the Commercial 1750 and the Tread Vision, turns
            to face the floor beside the treadmill for strength, yoga and stretching classes, which is a large
            part of what iFIT and Peloton memberships include. If you only ever run, a fixed or tilting screen
            is fine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Remember that the screen is usually the shortest-warranted part of the machine. NordicTrack
            covers the touchscreen on the current Commercial and T Series models for one year, against two to
            ten years on other parts. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains what to check, and <a href="/treadmill-console-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our console troubleshooting guide</a> covers what to do when a screen goes blank.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="without-subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Does It Work Without a Subscription?</h2>
<p>
            Every treadmill here runs without a membership in the sense that the belt moves and you can set
            speed and incline. What differs is how much of the screen does anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>iFIT (NordicTrack).</strong> NordicTrack says manual mode is always available: you start
            the belt and set speed and incline yourself, and the motor, deck and incline need no membership.
            Classes, automatic speed and incline control, Google Maps routes and most of the touchscreen's
            other content need a paid iFIT plan, and streaming apps, where offered, need the top plan plus your
            own streaming account. Our <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a> and <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a> go into the detail, and <a href="/can-you-watch-netflix-on-nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our Netflix guide</a> covers streaming.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton.</strong> Peloton's page says an All-Access Membership is required to access all
            Peloton content and applicable features, sold separately from the treadmill. Peloton also says
            its Just Run feature on the Tread Vision does not need the membership, so you can set speed and
            incline manually and see basic metrics. Classes, Run Analysis and the camera features need
            All-Access. Our <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Tread guide</a> sets out the terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Echelon.</strong> Independent reviews say the Stride treadmills run in a manual mode but need
            at least a free Echelon account, and that classes and most screen features need Premier. Echelon's
            warranty is also longer while Premier stays active; see our <a href="/echelon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Echelon buyer's guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bowflex JRNY.</strong> Bowflex says the T16 works without a JRNY subscription and a free
            JRNY account unlocks some programs. Its screen also runs Netflix, Prime Video, YouTube, Spotify and
            a web browser with your own accounts, and nine built-in programs, so it does more without a
            membership than iFIT or Peloton screens.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole.</strong> The F80's SOLE+ app is free and nothing on the treadmill needs a membership.
            The screen offers Wi-Fi, Bluetooth speakers and screen mirroring from your own device. If the idea
            of a monthly fee is what puts you off, start here, and see our full list of <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="membership-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Counting the Membership in the Price</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill with a class screen is really two purchases: the machine and the platform. Over the
            five to ten years a good treadmill lasts, the membership can cost as much as the treadmill, so
            compare the total rather than the sticker price. We do not quote current membership prices on this
            page because they change and two makers' sites would not show them to us on the day we checked;
            look them up at checkout and multiply by the years you expect to use the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three rules of thumb help. First, if you would not pay for the classes, do not pay extra for a
            screen designed around them: a no-membership machine such as the Sole F80, or a cheaper treadmill
            and a tablet, will serve you better. Second, if you would pay for classes, pick the platform first
            and the treadmill second, because switching later means buying a new machine. Our <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a> is the place to start. Third, check whether the warranty depends on the membership, as Echelon's
            does, before counting on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Resale is the hidden cost. A used class treadmill sells more easily when the next owner can see
            that it works in manual mode and that the membership transfers cleanly. Keep your account details
            and receipts, and our <a href="/smart-treadmill-vs-regular/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart vs regular treadmill guide</a> covers how smart features age.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tablet-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Built-In Screen or Your Own Tablet?</h2>
<p>
            A built-in touchscreen has real advantages: the treadmill can change its own speed and incline from
            a class, there is nothing to set up each session, and it is the right size and angle. It also has
            costs. It adds hundreds of dollars to the price, it is often the first part to fail and the
            shortest-warranted, and it locks you into one company's software, which may stop being updated
            long before the motor wears out.
          </p>
          <p>
            A tablet on a good holder gives you any app you like, from Zwift to Netflix, on a screen you can
            replace or upgrade, and many treadmills now pair over Bluetooth so apps can read your speed. What it
            usually cannot do is control the treadmill, except on machines and apps that support it. If you
            mostly want something to watch, a tablet is cheaper and more flexible; see our <a href="/best-treadmill-tablet-holders/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill tablet holder picks</a> and <a href="/best-treadmill-workout-apps/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workout apps</a>.
            If you want a trainer to drive the treadmill for you, a built-in screen is worth paying for.
          </p>
          <p>
            Searching for a "treadmill screen" sometimes means a replacement console rather than a new
            treadmill. Replacement touchscreens are model-specific parts sold by the maker's parts department;
            have your model and serial number ready, check whether the screen is still under warranty, and try
            the steps in our <a href="/treadmill-console-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">console troubleshooting guide</a> first, since a frozen screen is often a software or power problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Skip the Screen</h2>
<p>
            Skip a built-in touchscreen if you run to podcasts or music, if you already use Zwift or another
            app on your own device, if you want the longest possible warranty for the money, or if you are
            buying on a tight budget, where the same money buys a better motor and deck without a screen. Our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart treadmills</a> guide covers the platforms in more depth, and <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our Zwift treadmill picks</a> suit runners who bring their own screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            Choose a screen if you will follow classes several times a week, if you need on-screen guidance
            to keep going, or if the treadmill is shared by a household that wants different programs. In that
            case, buy the platform you will keep paying for, on the largest screen that fits your budget and
            your room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill With a Screen</h2>
          <p>The screen is the most visible part of a smart treadmill and the easiest to overpay for. These are the usual errors.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying the screen and not the treadmill</h3>
              <p>A big touchscreen on a short belt, a low weight rating or a weak warranty is a poor buy. Check the deck, motor rating, user weight and warranty first, then compare screens among machines that pass.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaving the membership out of the price</h3>
              <p>On iFIT, Peloton and Echelon machines, most of what the screen does depends on a monthly plan. Over several years it can cost as much as the treadmill. Count it, or choose a machine like the Sole F80 that needs none.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming the screen plays any app</h3>
              <p>Many treadmill touchscreens are locked to their maker’s software. Netflix on a NordicTrack needs iFIT’s top plan, and Peloton and Echelon screens are built around their own classes. If streaming your own apps matters, check the app list for the exact model before buying.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying an older model with the same name</h3>
              <p>NordicTrack in particular has sold several generations under the same names with different screens, motors and ratings. Check the model number on any discounted or clearance unit against the figures you are relying on.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The best treadmill with a screen is the one whose screen you will use
          without resenting the fee.</strong> For no membership at all, the Sole F80's 10.1-inch touchscreen at
          $1,599.99; for iFIT classes on a budget, the NordicTrack T Series 10 bought on a retailer discount;
          for streaming your own apps, the Bowflex T16; for the biggest screen, the Peloton Tread Vision.
          </p>
          <p>
            Check the deck, rating and warranty before the screen, count the membership in the price, and if you
          only want something to watch, consider a cheaper treadmill and a tablet. Our <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best smart treadmills</a> guide covers the platforms in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

